{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:25:25.809Z","sessionId":"1a64884c-3e4a-4736-8120-e44db2d3bffb","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Assistant:** Hook note: one entry in the error log. The memory compiler has 2 logs in backlog. Fix: run `compile.py` by hand later.\n\nUsing dev-sync to sync this Mac with GitHub.\n\n**Assistant:** Two transcript logs conflict. Logs keep both sides. Let me look.\n\n**Assistant:** Union merge keeps every line from both machines.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the DEV inventory.\n\n**Assistant:** Only `CommandCentre/server.out` (a log file) is dirty. Now fetch and merge every repo.\n\n**Assistant:** Folder name with a space broke one step. Redo that one.\n\n**Assistant:** Now: repos on GitHub with no local folder, and the activity ranking.\n\n**Assistant:** One repo is on GitHub but not here: `TOS_Evidence_Brief`. Clone it.\n\n**Assistant:** Now verify: re-walk every repo.\n\n**Assistant:** The one dirty item is this session's own live log in `~/.claude`. That is normal.\n\nStep 5. The active repo with the newest commit is `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`.\n\n**Assistant:** Your Mac now matches GitHub. Nothing was lost.\n\n**What I did**\n- DEV root: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV`\n- Config and skills: I pulled all three repos and pushed them. Skills are synced to Codex.\n- Memory: Windows had changed two log files, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md` and `2026-09-22.md`. I kept every line from both machines.\n- I checked 146 repos:\n  - I pulled 2: `_INBOX` got 1 commit and `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` got 2.\n  - I pushed 1: `CommandCentre`, which had only a changed log file, `server.out`.\n  - I cloned 1: `TOS_Evidence_Brief`. You made it on Windows yesterday.\n  - I created no new GitHub repos. No merge conflicts happened, so there are no `.from-*` files for you to fix.\n- 22 repos are active. 117 are dormant, and all of them are clean.\n- The only change left is this session's own log in `~/.claude`. That is normal.\n\n**Result: clean.**\n\n**Also: a system warning.** The memory compiler has 2 logs it has not processed yet. Run `compile.py` by hand when you have time.\n\n**Picking up: `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`** (the active repo with the newest commit)\n- **Last session:** 2026-09-21, Mac. The 06:27 commit today is only the Windows sync.\n- **Where things stand:** The Mac owns the GPS database and pulls at 10:00 and 16:30. The last pull was yesterday at 16:32 and it worked.\n- **Blocked on:** One decision is yours. Should the Mac wake at 16:28 so the Perch and VALD pulls run on time?\n  - Option 1: set the wake with `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`. To undo it: `sudo pmset repeat cancel`.\n  - Option 2: leave it. The data still arrives, but up to one hour late.\n  - I would pick option 1. It is one command and easy to undo.\n- **Next:**\n  - Find the job on Windows that still pulls GPS data, and stop it. Two writers make two databases that do not match.\n  - Today at 10:00, check the first pull.\n\nDo you want to continue with this, or do something else?\n\n**User:** I believe the job has been stopped on windows, you can go with option 2 and leave it.\n\n**Assistant:** I will write both decisions down, so the Windows laptop sees them too.\n\n**Assistant:** I wrote down both decisions and pushed them to GitHub. The Windows laptop gets them at its next sync.\n\n**What I did**\n- In `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`:\n  - The 16:28 wake is closed. Its entry now says you chose to leave it. Perch and VALD can land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps.\n  - The Windows GPS job is marked as stopped, based on your report. I cannot check the Windows laptop from the Mac.\n- In `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`: I updated the GPS line to match.\n\n**What to do now**\n- Nothing today. The next GPS pull runs at 10:00 by itself.\n- Change your mind about the wake only if data goes missing, not if it is just late. The command is in STATUS.md.\n\n**User:** <command-message>closeout</command-message>\n<command-name>/closeout</command-name>\n\n**User:** Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/closeout\n\n# Closeout\n\nOne command that replaces the manual end-of-day sequence: \"any loose ends?\" → \"commit and push what needs to be\" → \"is this in memory?\" → \"write a handoff so Codex can pick this up.\"\n\nRun all five checks, then give ONE verdict. Do not ask permission between steps — the invocation of this skill IS the authorization for the full sequence. Commit and push is standing-authorized for every DEV repo: each repo's `AGENTS.md` Session routine says \"update STATUS, commit, push at end\", and unpushed work on one machine is the top cause of conflicts on the other. The only exceptions are repos whose `CLAUDE.md` says their data must stay local (DexaProject is the precedent); commit those locally and list them as open.\n\n## Step 1 — Loose-ends sweep\n\n- `git status` across **every** repo in DEV, not only the ones named in this conversation. Another session, another harness, or the other machine may have left work dirty, and a closeout that only checks what it remembers touching will say \"clear to sign off\" over someone else's uncommitted work. Walk `DEV/`, `DEV/Football/` and `DEV/SpecialRequests/`, and report any repo with uncommitted or unpushed commits. The full walk takes about 90 seconds.\n  - Windows: `git -C <repo> status --porcelain` per repo. Do NOT use `Get-ChildItem -Recurse` to look for files; it silently skips paths past the 260-character limit. Inside a repo, `git ls-files` is the reliable way to ask whether a file exists.\n  - Repos with dirty state you did not touch: commit and push them like any other, and name them in the report so the user knows another session left them.\n- Unfinished todos/tasks from this session (TaskList if available).\n- Background jobs or workflows still running.\n- Anything the user was promised earlier in the session that wasn't delivered.\n\n## Step 2 — Commit & push\n\n- Every repo touched this session: stage, commit with a descriptive message, `git pull --no-rebase` (the other machine may have pushed), push. If the merge conflicts, follow the conflict steps in `dev-sync/SKILL.md` (they are the one home for them), then push and list the file under \"Still open\".\n- Before staging, make sure any new file over 95 MB and any `.env` is in `.gitignore`.\n- Local-only repos (CLAUDE.md says so): commit, do not push, list it.\n- Also check the three config repos, `~/.claude` (dotclaude), `~/.claude/skills` (claude-skills) and `~/.claude/global-memory` (claude-global-memory). If any has changes, commit and push it the same way, so the other machine's `/dev-sync` picks it up.\n- **Push `global-memory` even on a light session.** `daily-backup` pushes it nightly, which is too late for a same-day handoff: work the Mac in the afternoon, sit down at Windows that evening, and anything written to MEMORY.md or handoffs is still only on the Mac. Pushing it here is what makes memory available to the other machine tonight rather than tomorrow. It is the only repo that carries memory across, so treat an unpushed `global-memory` the same as an unpushed project repo: it blocks \"clear to sign off\".\n- Nothing to commit → say so in one line, move on.\n\n## Step 3 — Memory verification\n\n- Check `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` — must be empty. If not, surface contents and fix the hook issue if obvious.\n- Confirm today's transcript file exists at `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/<today>.md` and was modified within the last few hours.\n- Run the dev-docs harvest so repo docs reach memsearch: `bash ~/.claude/scripts/harvest-dev-docs.sh` (Mac) or `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $HOME\\.claude\\scripts\\harvest-dev-docs.ps1` (Windows). It copies every repo's `docs/*.md` into `~/.claude/global-memory/dev-docs/`, which is on the index list. One line of output; do this after Step 4 so today's STATUS is included.\n- If this session produced a durable decision or new active thread, update `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` (respect the 2,500-char cap; consolidate before adding) or write a Tier A memory file per the memory-system rules in CLAUDE.md.\n- **Tier A does not cross machines — mirror it or lose it.** Project memory lives under a slug built from the project's absolute path, and that path differs on the Mac and the Windows laptop, so a Tier A file written today is not auto-injected tomorrow on the other machine. Global memory and the repo both cross; Tier A does not. So for every Tier A fact written this session, ask: is this durable and project-scoped? If yes, it must also land in the repo — `docs/STATUS.md` for current state, `docs/DECISIONS.md` for a decision and its reasoning, the dated handoff for the detail. Treat Tier A as a fast local cache and the repo as the record. If a fact only ever exists in Tier A, assume the other machine will never see it.\n\n## Step 4 — Cross-harness handoff\n\nThis is the **canonical durable handoff** — the one future sessions and other harnesses read. (For a mid-session, chat-only summary before `/clear`, that's the `session-handoff` skill; the mattpocock `handoff` skill writes to the OS temp dir and is not durable — don't rely on it for cross-session state.)\n\nIf real work happened this session (not just Q&A), write a handoff to\n`<repo>/docs/handoffs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>.md` (or `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/` if no repo fits):\n\n- What was being worked on and why\n- Decisions made (with the reasoning that would be lost)\n- Exact file paths of everything created or modified this session\n- Next actions, most important first\n- Anything mid-flight (open questions, awaited inputs)\n\nPlain markdown, no harness-specific syntax — Codex, Antigravity, or a fresh Claude session must be able to resume from it. Commit it with the Step 2 commit when in a repo.\n\n**Always, in every repo touched (even for light sessions): update `docs/STATUS.md`.** It is the file the next session reads first, on either machine, in any harness. Rewrite the sections in place, keep it short:\n\n- `## Current state` — two to five lines on where things stand today\n- `## Next` — most important first\n- `## Open questions` — decisions waiting on Eric\n- `## Gotchas` — anything that will bite the next session\n\nThe dated handoff file is the detailed record; STATUS.md is the summary. Do Step 4 before the Step 2 commit so both land in the same push.\n\n## Step 5 — Verdict\n\nEnd with a single short report:\n\n```\n## Closeout — <date>\n\n**Committed & pushed:** <repos + one-line summary, or \"nothing to commit\">\n**STATUS.md:** updated in <repos>\n**Memory:** captured ✓ / issue found: <what>\n**Handoff:** <path, or \"not needed — no substantive work\">\n**Still open:** <numbered list, or \"none\">\n\nClear to sign off. / Not clear: <the one thing blocking>.\n```\n\n\"Clear to sign off\" means: every touched repo is pushed (or is a declared local-only repo). If anything is unpushed, the verdict is \"Not clear\" and the unpushed repo is the one thing blocking.\n\nEvery file created or modified during the session must appear somewhere in the report with its full path — \"where did this save?\" should never need to be asked.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Push every touched DEV repo. Do not leave the laptop with unpushed work; the other machine pulls from GitHub, not from this disk.\n- Never skip Step 3 — memory capture failing silently is the user's top recurring worry.\n- If a check fails and the fix is quick and safe (restage a file, clear a stale lock), fix it and note it. If the fix is risky, list it under \"Still open\" instead.\n- Total output should fit on one screen. The user is leaving; brevity is the deliverable.\n\n**Assistant:** Two new things turned up. Let me look.\n\n**Assistant:** It is Baylor work, so it is \"institutional\". I will copy the entry for `speed-velocity-profiling`.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the compiler backlog warning.\n\n**Assistant:** Running `compile.py` costs money (about $1.28 last run). I will leave it for you, not run it myself. I will remove only the backup line, because that problem is fixed.\n\n**Assistant:** Now I add a short \"Current state\" section to the GPS status file.\n\n**Assistant:** ## Closeout — 2026-09-22\n\n**Committed and pushed**\n- `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`: the two decisions (no wake, Windows job stopped), and a new \"Current state\" section.\n- `graphify-out`: 13 changed files. The nightly graph job made them, not this session.\n- `~/.claude`: the backup list now includes `TOS_Evidence_Brief`. Before, the 09:05 backup reported an error because the list did not have it. The old list is saved as `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`.\n- `~/.claude/global-memory`: the Windows log merge, the GPS memory line, and the removal of the fixed backup error.\n- `CommandCentre`: the `server.out` log (pushed during the sync).\n\n**STATUS.md:** I updated `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`.\n\n**Memory:** Saved. I updated `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` and today's log exists. I also copied the docs from all 140 repos into the memory search folder.\n\n**Handoff:** Not needed. This session was a sync and two small decisions.\n\n**Other files I changed**\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/2026-09-21.md` and `2026-09-22.md` (the merged logs)\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone)\n\n**Still open**\n1. The memory compiler still has old logs to process. Its warning is still in `.errors.log`. A run costs about $1.28, so I did not start it. Run it when you want. The command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\n\nClear to sign off. The one change I did not commit is this session's own live log in `~/.claude`. That is normal."}
